.TH shiftpaper-transition 1  shiftpaper 
.SH NAME
shiftpaper\-transition \- Show or change how one wallpaper changes into the next
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBshiftpaper transition\fR [\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-\-secs\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fINAME\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Show or change how one wallpaper changes into the next.
.PP
Applies to every change of wallpaper, from `set` or a slideshow, and a running daemon picks it up straight away. With no arguments, prints the current transition and how long it takes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-secs\fR \fI<SECS>\fR
How long it takes, in seconds, like 3 or 1.5
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
[\fINAME\fR]
The transition to use
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
sweep\-in: A wave sweeps into the scene: the new wallpaper\*(Aqs nearest things appear first, in front of the old one, and it fills in towards the distance
.IP \(bu 2
sweep\-out: A wave sweeps out of the scene: the old wallpaper\*(Aqs background gives way to the new one first, and its nearest things go last
.IP \(bu 2
morph: Everything changes together, the shape of the scene a little ahead of its colours
.IP \(bu 2
dissolve: The new wallpaper grows like spheres in the scene from random places, further things tending to go first
.IP \(bu 2
portal: The new wallpaper grows out from the cursor like a sphere in the scene, and follows the cursor if it moves
.IP \(bu 2
tide\-in: The new wallpaper rises through the old like a tide coming in, lowest places first
.IP \(bu 2
tide\-out: The old wallpaper drains away like a tide going out, uncovering the new one from the highest places down
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
shiftpaper transition                Print the current transition
shiftpaper transition portal         Use portal
shiftpaper transition tide\-in \-s 5   Use tide\-in, taking 5 seconds
shiftpaper transition \-\-secs 1.5     Keep the transition, change its length
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
shiftpaper(1)
.PP
